      <Id>TH297</Id>
      <ShortTitle>An HTTP-triggered Azure Function published with anonymous auth level, or with a long-lived function/host key, exposes the function logic to public invocation</ShortTitle>
      <Category>T</Category>
      <Description>Azure Functions support multiple authorization levels for HTTP triggers: anonymous, function (function-scoped key), admin (host-scoped key). Anonymous functions are reachable without any credential. Function and host keys are long-lived shared secrets that, if leaked through CI logs, public repositories, or browser network traces, grant the same access as the original publisher. Attackers can invoke the function repeatedly, modify downstream state through it, or use it as a pivot into systems the function reaches.</Description>
      <PropertiesMetaData>
        <ThreatMetaDatum>
          <Name>UserThreatShortDescription</Name>
          <Label>Short Description</Label>
          <HideFromUI>true</HideFromUI>
          <Values>
            <Value xsi:nil="true" />
          </Values>
          <Id>22222222-2222-2222-2222-222222222222</Id>
          <AttributeType>1</AttributeType>
        </ThreatMetaDatum>
        <ThreatMetaDatum>
          <Name>UserThreatDescription</Name>
          <Label>Description</Label>
          <HideFromUI>false</HideFromUI>
          <Values>
            <Value>Azure Functions support multiple authorization levels for HTTP triggers: anonymous, function (function-scoped key), admin (host-scoped key). Anonymous functions are reachable without any credential. Function and host keys are long-lived shared secrets that, if leaked through CI logs, public repositories, or browser network traces, grant the same access as the original publisher. Attackers can invoke the function repeatedly, modify downstream state through it, or use it as a pivot into systems the function reaches.</Value>
          </Values>
          <Id>22222222-2222-2222-2222-222222222222</Id>
          <AttributeType>0</AttributeType>
        </ThreatMetaDatum>
        <ThreatMetaDatum>
          <Name>Priority</Name>
          <Label>Severity</Label>
          <HideFromUI>false</HideFromUI>
          <Values>
            <Value>High</Value>
          </Values>
          <Description>Priority</Description>
          <Id>22222222-2222-2222-2222-222222222222</Id>
          <AttributeType>1</AttributeType>
        </ThreatMetaDatum>
        <ThreatMetaDatum>
          <Name>PossibleMitigations</Name>
          <Label>Possible Mitigation(s)</Label>
          <HideFromUI>false</HideFromUI>
          <Values>
            <Value>Avoid anonymous auth for any function that has side effects or accesses sensitive data. Prefer Microsoft Entra authentication via App Service Authentication ('Easy Auth') or function-level token validation rather than function/host keys. Where keys are used, treat them as secrets, store in Key Vault, rotate regularly, and never log them. Place HTTP functions behind APIM or Front Door with WAF for additional inspection.</Value>
          </Values>
          <Description>List of possbile mitigations.</Description>
          <Id>PossibleMitigations</Id>
          <AttributeType>2</AttributeType>
        </ThreatMetaDatum>
        <ThreatMetaDatum>
          <Name>SDLPhase</Name>
          <Label>SDL Phase</Label>
          <HideFromUI>false</HideFromUI>
          <Values>
            <Value>Design</Value>
          </Values>
          <Description>The Security Development Lifecycle Phase to be implemented.</Description>
          <Id>SDLPhase</Id>
          <AttributeType>1</AttributeType>
        </ThreatMetaDatum>
        <ThreatMetaDatum>
          <Name>Documentation</Name>
          <Label>Documentation</Label>
          <HideFromUI>false</HideFromUI>
          <Values>
            <Value>https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-functions/security-concepts</Value>
          </Values>
          <Description>Link to documentation</Description>
          <Id>Documentation</Id>
          <AttributeType>0</AttributeType>
        </ThreatMetaDatum>
        <ThreatMetaDatum>
          <Name>Steps</Name>
          <Label>Steps</Label>
          <HideFromUI>false</HideFromUI>
          <Values>
            <Value>For each HTTP-triggered function, document the auth level, key storage location and rotation policy, and any upstream gateway. Confirm anonymous and key-based access are justified for the function's risk class.</Value>
          </Values>
          <Description>Perscriptive guidance on how to implement mitigations.</Description>
          <Id>Steps</Id>
          <AttributeType>2</AttributeType>
        </ThreatMetaDatum>
        <ThreatMetaDatum>
          <Name>Effort</Name>
          <Label>Effort</Label>
          <HideFromUI>false</HideFromUI>
          <Values>
            <Value>Medium</Value>
          </Values>
          <Description>The level of effort to apply mitigations.</Description>
          <Id>Effort</Id>
          <AttributeType>1</AttributeType>
        </ThreatMetaDatum>
