      <Id>TH353</Id>
      <ShortTitle>AWS VPC peering and Transit Gateway routing extend network reachability in ways that may bypass intended trust boundaries between VPCs and accounts</ShortTitle>
      <Category>I</Category>
      <Description>VPC peering connections are non-transitive but pairwise routable; Transit Gateway hubs centralize routing across many VPCs and on-premises connections. Adding a new peering or TGW attachment, or modifying a route table to advertise wider CIDRs, can silently extend reachability between VPCs that were intended to be isolated. Cross-account peerings or TGW shares broaden the change-control surface to multiple account owners.</Description>
      <PropertiesMetaData>
        <ThreatMetaDatum>
          <Name>UserThreatShortDescription</Name>
          <Label>Short Description</Label>
          <HideFromUI>true</HideFromUI>
          <Values>
            <Value xsi:nil="true" />
          </Values>
          <Id>22222222-2222-2222-2222-222222222222</Id>
          <AttributeType>1</AttributeType>
        </ThreatMetaDatum>
        <ThreatMetaDatum>
          <Name>UserThreatDescription</Name>
          <Label>Description</Label>
          <HideFromUI>false</HideFromUI>
          <Values>
            <Value>VPC peering connections are non-transitive but pairwise routable; Transit Gateway hubs centralize routing across many VPCs and on-premises connections. Adding a new peering or TGW attachment, or modifying a route table to advertise wider CIDRs, can silently extend reachability between VPCs that were intended to be isolated. Cross-account peerings or TGW shares broaden the change-control surface to multiple account owners.</Value>
          </Values>
          <Id>22222222-2222-2222-2222-222222222222</Id>
          <AttributeType>0</AttributeType>
        </ThreatMetaDatum>
        <ThreatMetaDatum>
          <Name>Priority</Name>
          <Label>Severity</Label>
          <HideFromUI>false</HideFromUI>
          <Values>
            <Value>High</Value>
          </Values>
          <Description>Priority</Description>
          <Id>22222222-2222-2222-2222-222222222222</Id>
          <AttributeType>1</AttributeType>
        </ThreatMetaDatum>
        <ThreatMetaDatum>
          <Name>PossibleMitigations</Name>
          <Label>Possible Mitigation(s)</Label>
          <HideFromUI>false</HideFromUI>
          <Values>
            <Value>Govern VPC peering and TGW route changes through change control involving the owners of the affected VPCs. Use Resource Access Manager (RAM) sharing audit to track cross-account TGW shares. Apply Network ACLs and security groups as defense in depth at the subnet boundary even within a TGW. Audit TGW route tables and peering connections on a defined cadence.</Value>
          </Values>
          <Description>List of possbile mitigations.</Description>
          <Id>PossibleMitigations</Id>
          <AttributeType>2</AttributeType>
        </ThreatMetaDatum>
        <ThreatMetaDatum>
          <Name>SDLPhase</Name>
          <Label>SDL Phase</Label>
          <HideFromUI>false</HideFromUI>
          <Values>
            <Value>Design</Value>
          </Values>
          <Description>The Security Development Lifecycle Phase to be implemented.</Description>
          <Id>SDLPhase</Id>
          <AttributeType>1</AttributeType>
        </ThreatMetaDatum>
        <ThreatMetaDatum>
          <Name>Documentation</Name>
          <Label>Documentation</Label>
          <HideFromUI>false</HideFromUI>
          <Values>
            <Value>https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/peering/what-is-vpc-peering.html</Value>
          </Values>
          <Description>Link to documentation</Description>
          <Id>Documentation</Id>
          <AttributeType>0</AttributeType>
        </ThreatMetaDatum>
        <ThreatMetaDatum>
          <Name>Steps</Name>
          <Label>Steps</Label>
          <HideFromUI>false</HideFromUI>
          <Values>
            <Value>Diagram VPC peering and TGW topology including cross-account attachments. For each transit path, confirm both endpoints' owners agree the path is intended.</Value>
          </Values>
          <Description>Perscriptive guidance on how to implement mitigations.</Description>
          <Id>Steps</Id>
